WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:04.320
Who would have thought that a toilet&nbsp;
could have inspired such an emotional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.340
scene? You might need to keep a roll&nbsp;
handy to wipe … away those tears.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:13.920
The regeneration episodes of "Doctor Who" always&nbsp;
provide a harrowing goodbye worth having a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.600
weep over. But none have touched the nerve&nbsp;
of a fanbase quite like the Tenth Doctor's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.280
farewell with Rose Tyler. Often considered one&nbsp;
of the best "Doctor Who" companions of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.300
Rose's time-travel journey came to an end&nbsp;
in the second series of the "Doctor Who"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:32.220
relaunch. In the 2006 episode "Doomsday,"&nbsp;
the Doctor and Rose are seen hanging on for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.760
dear life after the Cybermen break&nbsp;
through a fracture in the universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.660
Following a prolonged fight against&nbsp;
the scary metal men, plus the Daleks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:44.640
Rose falls through the fracture and is separated&nbsp;
from the Doctor forever. Cue the tissues.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:50.040
It's not until their final moments together that&nbsp;
the true heartbreak reveals itself. The Doctor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:55.560
manages to send an image of himself to visit&nbsp;
Rose on a beach, in true romantic style. The pair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:01.440
exchange teary goodbyes, with the Doctor vanishing&nbsp;
before sharing his parting words. SFX Magazine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:07.500
voted this scene as the greatest sci-fi moment of&nbsp;
all time back in 2014, with fans deeply moved by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:12.780
the duo's painful goodbyes. Addressing the episode&nbsp;
10 years later, director Graeme Harper said,

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:16.560
"Just as he starts to fade away from&nbsp;
this dimension never quite able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.320
get the words 'I love you!'&nbsp;
out, he disappears completely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:23.460
leaving a devastated Rose — and&nbsp;
breaking all our hearts on her behalf."

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:28.347
"Rose Tyler…"

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:32.640
Dedicated fans might have a difficult&nbsp;
time dealing with any character's death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.720
but when their demise comes completely out&nbsp;
of the blue, the fallout can be unbearable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.880
Such was the case for the Season 3 death&nbsp;
of Lexa in The CW sci-fi series "The 100."

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:45.780
After being hit by a stray bullet,&nbsp;
Lexa loses too much blood and dies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:50.520
shortly after. As Clarke happens to be The CW's&nbsp;
first official openly bisexual main character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:54.480
the decision to kill off the first woman&nbsp;
audiences see her have a relationship with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:02:01.080
was immediately questioned by viewers. As noted&nbsp;
by Culturess, LGBTQIA+ characters tend to bear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:06.300
the brunt of unnecessary on-screen death, and&nbsp;
many fans felt Lexa was just another victim.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:10.560
In fact, the fan reaction became so&nbsp;
volatile that "The 100" bosses had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.440
to officially apologize. As reported by E! Online,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:18.600
executive producer Jason Rothenberg second-guessed&nbsp;
his decision in an open letter to fans, sharing,

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.540
"Their relationship held greater&nbsp;
importance than even I realized.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:25.440
And that very important representation&nbsp;
was taken away by one stray bullet."

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.078
It's a scathing mark on an&nbsp;
otherwise successful show.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.980
It might have reignited the&nbsp;
world's love for Kate Bush,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:36.540
but Max and Vecna's standoff was enough to&nbsp;
make any "Stranger Things" fan hold their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:40.560
breath. After the suspense of Max escaping&nbsp;
Vecna's clutches at the end of "Volume One"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.000
of Season 4, the final episode proved to&nbsp;
be another punch to the gut for viewers.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:50.000
"I thought we lost you."
"I'm still here. I'm still here."

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.780
Choosing to offer herself up as bait to distract&nbsp;
Vecna long enough for his physical body to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:59.520
killed, Max's second pass at levitation ends&nbsp;
in snapping limbs and blood streaming from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:04.440
her eyes. This is haunting enough without the&nbsp;
added shock of watching a fan favorite fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:08.580
at the final hurdle. The episode offers&nbsp;
lots of emotion, but very few answers.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:13.020
Even though the second part of Season 4 has&nbsp;
since wrapped up, fans are still mulling the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:18.180
scene over in great detail, including Eleven&nbsp;
using her powers to revive Max. Some viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:22.740
have stated that Sadie Sink herself deserved&nbsp;
better, while several fan theories speculate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:26.460
on what the Duffer Brothers might have in&nbsp;
store next. What's more, Redditors believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:31.518
that this could only be the start of Max's&nbsp;
problems, fueling rumors about Season 5.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:36.420
It's an age-old tale — a vampire falls for&nbsp;
a sweet vampire slayer, and the vampire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:42.000
slayer has to kill the vampire in an unsettling&nbsp;
surprise. In "Buffy the Vampire Slayer" Season 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.980
Angel reverts back into his evil persona&nbsp;
of Angelus. One thing leads to another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:52.140
and his soul is restored, which turns him back&nbsp;
into the benevolent Angel ... just in time for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:57.060
Buffy to have to kill him in order to close a hell&nbsp;
dimension and save the world. Throughout the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:01.620
Buffy has to make a lot of noble sacrifices&nbsp;
for the greater good. It's kind of her thing.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:05.280
Their final farewell -- for the time being --&nbsp;
is just as heartbreaking as you would expect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:09.240
with some fans still considering the scene&nbsp;
as one of the show's most emotional. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:13.140
seconds prior to Angel's death, Buffy tells&nbsp;
him to close his eyes before pushing him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:17.220
into hell. She is then left in tears while&nbsp;
trying to process what she has had to do.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:21.840
In the finale of Marvel's TV sensation&nbsp;
"WandaVision," the tables turn for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.340
superhero people may have started to feel contempt&nbsp;
for. Throughout the show's standalone season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:30.840
Wanda uses her Scarlet Witch powers to take&nbsp;
an entire town hostage to create the normal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:36.000
life she has always dreamed of, turning from&nbsp;
a celebrated Avenger to a selfish villain. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:39.960
has been through quite a few traumas by this&nbsp;
point in her story: the death of her parents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:44.280
her brother, and her android boyfriend&nbsp;
on separate occasions. But even Agatha&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:48.180
Harkness pales in comparison when it comes&nbsp;
to conniving plans that hurt many people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:53.700
Wanda realizes how far over the edge she's&nbsp;
gone in Episode 8, "Previously On," which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:58.800
includes a flashback of Vision delivering&nbsp;
his now-famous "Love Persevering" speech.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:04.740
"But what is grief? If not love persevering."

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:09.300
This is the episode when Wanda begins to&nbsp;
see how dangerous she really can be. Fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:13.740
quickly took to social media to express their&nbsp;
heartbreak and emotion after the episode aired,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:18.540
with Wanda's realization connecting with the lies&nbsp;
told by SWORD head honcho Tyler Hayward. It's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:23.220
lot to take in for any dedicated fan, but the&nbsp;
sadness might just be worth the payoff. Viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:27.480
commented that Wanda could now perhaps be seen&nbsp;
as one of the strongest Avengers in the MCU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:31.058
giving her an emotional turning&nbsp;
point that's both flawed and human.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:36.060
Preventing the end of the world is no light&nbsp;
feat, and sometimes ultimate sacrifices become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:41.880
necessary. In "The Umbrella Academy" Season 2's&nbsp;
"743," ghostly Ben leaves the comfort of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:46.020
spiritual world behind by using the last of his&nbsp;
energy to have a heart-to-heart with Victor. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:50.280
pair have one last emotional farewell before&nbsp;
Ben disappears into glimmering blue lights.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:53.820
While each of the siblings put forth&nbsp;
their best efforts to push ahead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:57.180
it's left up to the only non-living&nbsp;
human of the bunch to save the day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:00.960
His absence from "The Umbrella Academy"&nbsp;
had a noticeable impact on the group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:04.519
with fans acknowledging that it might&nbsp;
have simply been his time to move on.

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:10.200
You might be surprised to see Rick, and not&nbsp;
Morty, appear on this list. Sure, Morty has his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:15.360
fair share of tear-jerking moments; living out an&nbsp;
entire relationship, as well as a harrowing plane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:20.280
crash, before watching it erased by an accidental&nbsp;
timeline reset was soul-crushing for him, though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:25.440
many fans laughed and saw the humorous side. Yet&nbsp;
it's Rick that delivers a gut punch when he finds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:28.620
out his stranger-turned-nemesis&nbsp;
Tony the toilet guy has died.

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:32.220
In the episode "The Old Man and&nbsp;
the Seat," we see Rick's idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:36.240
a private toilet heaven interrupted by&nbsp;
an outsider. Intent on making him pay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.860
Rick goes above and beyond to take his revenge,&nbsp;
only for Tony to show him nothing but kindness.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:46.080
Seeing such a sweet and polite character last&nbsp;
for mere minutes on screen feels like a difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:51.360
watch in itself, but it's Rick's sudden emotional&nbsp;
compass that really clinches this as an upsetting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:55.860
moment for fans. As one Redditor explained, Rick&nbsp;
has always wanted connections that mean something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.860 --> 00:07:00.240
but he also sees this as a weak part&nbsp;
of his personality. In true Rick style,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:04.239
the friendship that never was still&nbsp;
makes him more bitter than compassionate.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:09.660
In a relatively unexpected move, the sensibility&nbsp;
of "Casablanca" crossed over into the hit series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:14.460
"Star Trek: The Next Generation." This specific&nbsp;
episode's existence is somewhat upsetting to fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:20.520
as it maintains a meager average score of&nbsp;
6.5 on IMDb. Even while the contents of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:24.660
"We'll Always Have Paris" failed to impress,&nbsp;
its final moment pulls at the heartstrings.

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:28.980
Fans learn that Picard and his ex Jenice&nbsp;
were originally supposed to meet at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:34.140
Parisian Café des Artistes in 2342,&nbsp;
but Picard never showed. Consumed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:37.500
with life on the ship rather than his&nbsp;
romantic relationship with Jenice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:40.800
Picard opts to part ways and the&nbsp;
two fail to track each other down.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:44.760
The emotional kicker comes when Picard&nbsp;
and Jenice finally get to have their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:47.700
reunion. The pair's chance to&nbsp;
say goodbye feels bittersweet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:52.560
as some fans suggest Picard isn't made out&nbsp;
to be a great guy after all. Redditors do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:56.280
agree that putting this relationship to bed&nbsp;
is charming and that it takes an emotional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:59.539
look at what happens when things don't&nbsp;
work out the way you hoped they would.

00:07:59.539 --> 00:08:00.527
"You're wonderful."

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:08.340
As far as "Supernatural" goes, "Swan Song" is&nbsp;
an episode that sets fans' emotional pulses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:13.080
racing. In the finale of "Supernatural" Season&nbsp;
5, Sam is assumed to have returned from hell to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:18.300
see something he never wanted to — a domesticated&nbsp;
Dean. The buildup to Sam's realization is deadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:23.880
with Lucifer already claiming the lives of Castiel&nbsp;
and Bobby. While Sam offers his body as a vessel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:27.840
Dean finds himself returning to Lisa.&nbsp;
The heartbreaking final scene shows Sam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:31.980
standing outside Lisa's home, watching the happy&nbsp;
family have dinner as if nothing had happened.

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:36.540
Emotions were already high before the final&nbsp;
scene aired, with Redditors commenting on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:41.580
the rollercoaster of feelings between Sam and&nbsp;
Dean in the fight to win back control. Yet the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:46.320
final moments cause a different kind of upset,&nbsp;
almost acting as a letdown to what came before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:50.700
Even while Dean assumed he was fulfilling&nbsp;
Sam's wishes of granting him a normal life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:54.720
the Winchester boys getting domesticated&nbsp;
goes completely against their hunting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:58.979
nature. Regardless, some agree that&nbsp;
it provides a perfectly teary ending.

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:04.740
When it came to its Season 3 ending, the producers&nbsp;
of "Misfits" decided to go out with a bang. One of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:09.480
the many catastrophic fallouts in the episode is&nbsp;
the death of Alisha in an instance of terrible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:14.520
timing. Alisha happens to walk in on an undead&nbsp;
Rachel during a revenge-oriented knife attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:19.440
on the Misfits. Rachel slashes Alisha's&nbsp;
throat and she bleeds out in Simon's arms.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:23.580
Back when it first aired in 2011,&nbsp;
Alisha's death made viewers sob into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:27.540
their sofa cushions. There has been&nbsp;
plenty of fan debate over which death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:31.140
in "Misfits" was truly the saddest,&nbsp;
and Alisha's often comes out on top.

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:36.120
According to one Redditor, her death is&nbsp;
so sad because of its layers. Future Simon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:39.660
likely knows what would end up happening&nbsp;
to her, and that means their relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:43.920
is stuck in an infinite loop. The rest of&nbsp;
the gang's reaction is difficult to watch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:48.660
with each of them functionally powerless and&nbsp;
unable to fix the situation. Regardless of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:52.140
popular opinion, Alisha's murder&nbsp;
is inarguably cold and callous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:56.119
and could have easily been prevented if she&nbsp;
was in the right place at the right time.

00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:02.580
"Star Trek: Voyager" loses a valuable member of&nbsp;
its crew in Episode 23 of Season 7. After finding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:07.080
a colony of Talaxians that he wishes to help,&nbsp;
Neelix develops feelings for Dexa and her son&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:11.880
Brax. This leaves him feeling conflicted after he&nbsp;
finishes his mission. While his reason for leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:16.740
is very much understandable, his departure is full&nbsp;
of emotion. With his fellow crew members lining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:21.060
the ship's hallways to see him off, Neelix leaves&nbsp;
Voyager with a trembling lip and a sense of hope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:26.280
for his newfound romance. Having never been an&nbsp;
official Starfleet officer himself, viewers were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:30.960
left unsure what kind of farewell Neelix would&nbsp;
get, and the answer was positively emotional.

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:33.540
Some fans have since commended the episode while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:37.380
others questioned why he had to choose&nbsp;
between romance and Voyager in the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:41.459
place. Executive producer Kenneth&nbsp;
Biller told Cinescape at the time,

00:10:41.459 --> 00:10:46.619
"We often said, 'What would happen if Neelix&nbsp;
ran into Talaxians and had to make a decision?'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.619 --> 00:10:51.360
I said very early on in the season, the only&nbsp;
way I would do that story was if Neelix left."

